  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/429c0a3eb7a6b1b0a99fba09eb7dd062f645669e99d39a919dc369467b4c373d.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-2,4]$ and differentiable on $(-2,4).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *",
  "worked_solution": "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-2$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-2)}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{1}{3}.$*",
